          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Inventions and Designs</strong><br>\nFulton continued to innovate, designing the world's first steam warship, the \"Demologos,\" and improving steamboat engines and hull designs. His work helped lay the groundwork for modern naval engineering.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Relationships</strong><br>\nFulton married Harriet Livingston in 1808, with whom he had four children. His relationships with other inventors and statesmen of the time, like Robert R. Livingston, were pivotal to his career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Lasting Influence</strong><br>\nFulton died in 1815 from tuberculosis. He left behind a legacy as a pioneer of steam navigation, influencing transportation technology worldwide. His name endures in the many schools, places, and inventions named after him.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
